        "metadata": {
            "kira_final": "Let me understand the issue better. The user mentioned they made changes to the OCaml garbage collector to improve sweeping performance by \"run-length compressing the free space in the major heap\". Looking at the code, I can see the current free list implementation already does some run-length encoding - it stores the number of contiguous free blocks in the wosize field of the free header.\n\nBut wait - the issue is that the compiler crashes during bootstrapping. Let me look more carefully at the crash. The crash happens in `do_some_marking` at line 1274 where it does `header_t hd = Hd_val(block);`. The block is coming from the prefetch buffer. The assertion `CAMLassert(Is_markable(block));` passes, but then accessing `Hd_val(block)` causes a segfault.\n\nThis suggests that the block pointer is invalid - it might be pointing to memory that's been freed or corrupted. The `Is_markable` check only verifies that it's a block (not an immediate) and not young. But it doesn't verify that the block is actually a valid, live object in the major heap.\n\nThe problem could be that during sweeping, free blocks are being added to the free list with `POOL_FREE_HEADER(0)` which sets the tag to `No_scan_tag` and color to `NOT_MARKABLE`. But the wosize field is used to store the run-length of contiguous free blocks.\n\nWait, I see the issue now. In the sweep code:\n```c\n*p = POOL_FREE_HEADER(0);\n```\nThis sets the header to have wosize=0, tag=No_scan_tag, color=NOT_MARKABLE.\n\nThen later, when merging c",
